{"cuisine/jiangxi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/jiangxi-cuisine","n":"Jiangxi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Jiangxi cuisine, also known as Gan cuisine (赣菜; 贛菜; gàncài), is a style of Chinese cuisine derived from the native cooking styles of Jiangxi province in southern China. According to the East China Travel Guide published in 1983, Jiangxi cuisine has its unique taste which can be described in four Chinese idioms: 原汁原味 (aim to bring out the own flavor of the cooking material)，油厚不腻 (dishes contained a significant amount of oil but the taste are not greasy)，口味浓厚 (dishes have really thick taste)，咸鲜兼辣 (always come with spicy solid, salty flavor). Jiangxi cuisine is widely popular within the ordinary family because most of the notable dishes from Jiangxi cuisine are the extension of homely dishes with solid local flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"jiangxi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiangxi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Madame Huang's Kitchen: Henan cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"carolynjphillips.blogspot.com","note":"Recipe posts on Jiangxi dishes from Carolyn Phillips' blog, Madame Huang's Kitchen.","url":"https://carolynjphillips.blogspot.com/search/label/Jiangxi%20cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"All Under Heaven: Recipes from the 35 Cuisines of China","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Carolyn Phillips, 2016","note":"Regional survey of Chinese cooking that includes a section on Jiangxi dishes.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/534780/all-under-heaven-by-carolyn-phillips/9781607749820/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781607749820"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/6c/Xiao_long_bao_closeup_in_Shanghai.jpg/500px-Xiao_long_bao_closeup_in_Shanghai.jpg","w":500,"h":447,"by":"MR+G from Shanghai","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiao_long_bao_closeup_in_Shanghai.jpg"}},"cuisine/quechua-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/quechua-foodways","n":"Quechua foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Andes of Peru, around Cusco","names":"Quechua","intro":[["For Quechua farmers in the Andes, the native ",["potato","ingredient/potato"]," is the most basic food. In the Potato Park near Pisaq, Cusco, six Quechua communities organized as ayllus look after about 1,400 kinds of potato and other tubers, along with ",["Quinoa","ingredient/quinoa"],", kiwicha (",["Amaranth","ingredient/amaranth"],"), tarwi, oca, olluco, corn and beans, and they raise ",["llamas","ingredient/llama"],", alpacas and guinea pigs. Families high up trade potatoes for quinoa and corn grown farther down."],["In the coldest months, potatoes are left out to freeze at night and dry in the sun until they're black and hard. That's ",["Chuño","ingredient/chuno"],", and it keeps for a decade or more. Moraya is freeze-dried in running water until it turns white. Fresh potatoes are boiled whole in their skins or baked in a watya, a small oven built of earth and heated with wood. At planting, ",["coca leaves","ingredient/coca-leaf"]," and llama fat go into the first hole as an offering to Pachamama."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cultural Survival, Quechua guardians of the potato","url":"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/fr/publications/cultural-survival-quarterly/quechua-guardians-potato"},{"name":"Asociación ANDES, Potato Park, Pisaq","url":"https://andes.org.pe/potato-park/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"quechua-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Potato Park, Pisaq","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"andes.org.pe","note":"Six Quechua communities near Cusco who look after some 1,400 kinds of native potato.","url":"https://andes.org.pe/potato-park/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Inca cuisine","cuisine/inca-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/flour-tortilla":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/flour-tortilla","n":"Flour tortilla","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A flour tortilla (, ) or wheat tortilla is a tortilla made from finely ground wheat flour. Made with flour- and water-based dough, it is pressed and cooked, similar to corn tortillas. The simplest recipes use only flour, water, fat, and salt, but commercially made flour tortillas generally contain chemical leavening agents such as baking powder, and other ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-flour-tortilla"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flour_tortilla"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Quesadilla","dish/quesadilla"],["Taco","dish/taco"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Taco","dish/taco"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Tortilla","ingredient/tortilla"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Adobada","ingredient/adobada"],["Bean","ingredient/bean"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Jalapeño","ingredient/jalapeno"],["Machaca","ingredient/machaca"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Salsa (sauce)","ingredient/salsa-sauce"],["Corn tortilla","ingredient/corn-tortilla"],["Protein_(nutrient)","ingredient/protein-nutrient"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/56/NCI_flour_tortillas.jpg/500px-NCI_flour_tortillas.jpg","w":500,"h":406,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NCI_flour_tortillas.jpg"}},"dish/corn-fritter":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/corn-fritter","n":"Corn fritter","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Corn fritters are fried cakes of a dough or batter made of, or containing, a featured quantity of corn kernels. Originating in Native American cuisine, they are a traditional sweet and savory snack in the Americas, the Southern United States, as well as Indonesia where they are known as perkedel jagung or bakwan jagung."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-corn-fritter"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corn_fritter"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Zea mays Saccharata Group","ingredient/zea-mays-saccharata-group"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dis